# doorbell only: the hop is the product's request descriptor (op=NEXT, seq, batch,
# data_bytes) and the worker's resp_seq/status. No data segment exists and no
# payload moves; MB/s is the descriptor's 72 bytes per hop, not payload.
# rows/s = hops/s x batch_rows: --batch-rows is what a real batch would have
# carried, so the conversion is honest arithmetic, not rows produced.
# CPU/hop is user+sys over both processes for the timed hops. The regipc engine
# spins up to 100 us per side before falling back to its semaphore, so its spin
# is in the CPU; the shm engine sleeps on a futex per handoff.
# The worker stops through the engine's post_request (the Python client's
# Ring_stop shape); the semaphore-only stop (fastpostgres_ring_stop) is noticed
# by a regipc worker only at its 100 ms wait_request timeout. teardown_ms in the
# per-run records is the stop's real cost, measured outside the timed hops.
### END TRANSPORT

# one table per method, the doorbell's rate
# median over 3 rep(s); min..max over the reps; us CPU/hop = (producer_cpu_s + consumer_cpu_s) x 1e6 / count

--- shm (the ring's own semaphores: a futex wake per handoff) ---
